    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've been having a major issue with my dark chocolate batches lately. Even when I follow my usual process, I'm getting this weird white, dull coating on the surface after it sets—like a fat bloom. Can you explain the science behind why this happens and what I'm doing wrong with my tempering?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white, dull coating is likely a form of sugar bloom or fat bloom that occurs when moisture or temperature fluctuations cause cocoa butter to migrate to the surface. This usually indicates that the cocoa butter crystals are not properly stabilized during the tempering process. To fix this, ensure your tempering is done precisely, maintaining consistent temperatures, and store your finished chocolate in a cool, dry environment away from drastic temperature swings.",
      "source": "target",
